Neobrix greeter recovery
========================

Read this before activating the greeter, not after. It is deliberately a separate
file: a ready-to-paste rollback command sitting in a comment inside config.toml
is too easy to run by mistake, and on the machine this was first built on exactly
that happened — a command in a config comment got executed and the greeter did
not come up after a reboot. Nothing in /etc/greetd/config.toml is runnable.


Getting a shell when there is no login screen
---------------------------------------------

Press Ctrl+Alt+F2 (F3..F6 work too). systemd-logind spawns a getty on those
virtual terminals on demand, independently of any display manager, so this works
even when greetd is broken or disabled. Log in there as your normal user.

If a graphical session is still running, Ctrl+Alt+F1 goes back to it.


Rolling back to the text-mode greeter
-------------------------------------

    sudo cp /etc/greetd/config.toml.pre-regreet /etc/greetd/config.toml
    sudo systemctl restart greetd

That restores whatever greetd was configured to run before Neobrix touched it.
`./install/deploy.sh --greeter` saves that file once, on its first run, and never
overwrites it afterwards, so it stays the original.

If it is missing, write an agreety config by hand — agreety is part of the greetd
package itself, so it cannot go missing the way a graphical greeter can:

    sudo tee /etc/greetd/config.toml >/dev/null <<'EOF'
    [terminal]
    vt = 1

    [default_session]
    command = "agreety --cmd /bin/fish"
    user = "greeter"
    EOF
    sudo systemctl restart greetd

Restarting greetd ends the running graphical session. Do it from a VT, not from a
terminal inside your desktop.


Putting the Neobrix greeter back
--------------------------------

    sudo cp /etc/greetd/config.toml.neobrix-greeter /etc/greetd/config.toml
    sudo systemctl restart greetd

Preview it first, without touching greetd at all — this renders the real UI with
no login path involved:

    regreet --demo -c /etc/greetd/regreet.toml -s /etc/greetd/regreet.css


Going back to the previous display manager
------------------------------------------

Activating greetd means disabling whatever display manager was enabled before.
`deploy.sh --greeter` records which one that was:

    cat /etc/greetd/PREVIOUS-DISPLAY-MANAGER

To reverse it (substituting that unit name):

    sudo systemctl disable --now greetd
    sudo systemctl enable --now sddm     # or the unit named in that file

Only one display manager may be enabled at a time; `systemctl enable` on a second
one fails, because they all contend for the same display-manager.service alias.

On the author's laptop the previous display manager was **SDDM**. It is disabled,
not uninstalled: disabling is reversible with one command, while uninstalling in
the middle of a greeter that will not start is not. Re-enabling it is the two
commands above.

If you do decide to remove it later, do it only after the new greeter has
survived several real reboots, and note that getting it back means installing the
package again before enabling it.

CachyOS's noctalia-greeter, which earlier versions of this file pointed at, is not
part of this picture — it was specific to the VM Neobrix was developed on.


Choosing the right session at the greeter
-----------------------------------------

Pick **Hyprland (uwsm-managed)**, not plain Hyprland. Neobrix's systemd user
units (neobrix-shell, hypridle, the polkit agent, cliphist) are all
WantedBy=graphical-session.target, and it is uwsm that reaches that target. Start
the plain session and Hyprland comes up with no bar, no panels and no idle
daemon. ReGreet remembers the choice per user afterwards, in /var/lib/regreet.


Things that are deliberately not offered
----------------------------------------

No autologin, and no greetd `initial_session`. The greeter is the only
authentication gate on this machine; `skip_selection` stays false. If the greeter
is failing, fix it or roll back — do not route around it by logging in
automatically.
